# .editorconfig sets `end_of_line = crlf`, and ADR-019 makes IDE0055 a build
# error — so a checkout that produces LF fails the build on every line the
# formatter inspects. On Windows `core.autocrlf=true` hides this by converting
# on the way out. On the Linux runner CI uses, nothing does, and the build goes
# red on a tree that is green on every developer's machine.
#
# That is not hypothetical: it is how PR-05 first went red. Nineteen IDE0055
# errors in one file, reproducible locally only by converting that file to LF,
# and invisible to `dotnet build` on Windows at any SDK version.
#
# `text eol=crlf` normalises to LF in the object store — where the blobs
# already are, so this renormalises nothing — and checks out CRLF everywhere,
# which is what .editorconfig has always asked for.
*.cs text eol=crlf

# Two file types override the rule above, and neither is a preference.
# A CRLF shebang makes the kernel look for an interpreter named `python3\r` —
# "bad interpreter", from a line that looks correct in every editor. The
# licence gate runs on that runner, and the .claude/scripts helpers run under
# Git Bash on Windows, where the same `\r` breaks bash mid-line.
*.py text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# The Helm tree is the same failure one artefact over, and it is worth stating
# how it was found. Go's template engine copies a template's bytes through, so
# a CRLF template renders a CR at the end of every emitted line — measured:
# `path: /health/live\r`. deploy/helm/smoke.sh then greps that output with
# patterns anchored on `$`, and on the Linux runner none of them match.
#
# Locally the same run is GREEN, because MSYS grep treats CRLF as the line
# terminator and strips the CR before matching. So the first measurement said
# the hazard did not exist and was wrong for the reason the .cs paragraph above
# gives — the platform hid it — and only reading the rendered bytes settled it.
#
# A checkout is what decides this, not an editor: with core.autocrlf=true these
# files arrive CRLF unless something says otherwise.
deploy/helm/** text eol=lf

# deploy/canary/ joins on the same argument as deploy/observability/ below, and
# for once ahead of paying for it. `deploy/helm/smoke.sh` greps canary.json,
# `canary.py` matches the alert thresholds out of a rules file, and the whole
# tree is read by gates on a Linux runner and edited on Windows. A tree that
# arrives CRLF on one machine and LF on another is a gate whose result depends
# on who checked it out. The .py files are already covered above; this is for
# canary.json and the README beside them, so the tree is one thing.
deploy/canary/** text eol=lf

# deploy/observability/ joins on the Helm tree's argument rather than on its
# own measurement, and the difference is worth stating. Nothing here is
# rendered through a template engine, so the CR-per-line failure above cannot
# happen in the same way — but these files are consumed by Prometheus, Grafana
# and k6 on Linux, and `check.py` matches PromQL against C# with anchored
# patterns. A tree that arrives CRLF on one machine and LF on another is a gate
# whose result depends on who checked it out, which is the property the .cs
# paragraph above spent a PR discovering.
deploy/observability/** text eol=lf
